00:08:55.580 --> 00:08:57.979
So now let's see what's
happening behind the scenes.

00:08:57.980 --> 00:09:04.800
So this is what CloudBerry
is capable of doing.

00:09:04.801 --> 00:09:08.650
Architectural wise,
CloudBerry is a middleware

00:09:08.651 --> 00:09:11.590
that sits on top of
your existing database.

00:09:11.591 --> 00:09:16.850
And, the one we saw just now,
the back end is using AsterixDB.

00:09:18.100 --> 00:09:21.490
Because we have a connector that
talks to the database using

00:09:21.491 --> 00:09:22.520
our native language.

00:09:22.521 --> 00:09:24.102
Two languages, we're bilingual.

00:09:24.103 --> 00:09:26.531
EQL and SQLS*Plus.

00:09:26.532 --> 00:09:29.337
And we also recently just
built one more connector or

00:09:29.338 --> 00:09:30.900
two more connectors.

00:09:30.901 --> 00:09:34.060
One talking to MySQL,
one talking to a [INAUDIBLE].

00:09:34.061 --> 00:09:37.080
Plus the requirement is your
database should provide or

00:09:37.081 --> 00:09:39.860
your backend, not database,
your backend should provide core

00:09:39.861 --> 00:09:43.750
language either through database
[INAUDIBLE] or RESTful API so

00:09:43.751 --> 00:09:46.835
that the middleware can talk to
you by issuing some queries.

00:09:46.836 --> 00:09:49.500
In this you will miss
some assumptions.

00:09:49.501 --> 00:09:53.820
One assumption is the data for
now is append only,

00:09:53.821 --> 00:09:57.340
once your data, because somehow
logic relies on assumption,

00:09:57.341 --> 00:10:00.860
once the data is already
inserted into your tables,

00:10:00.861 --> 00:10:02.220
we can not modify anymore.

00:10:03.220 --> 00:10:06.378
And we know a lot of cases
such as this condition,

00:10:06.379 --> 00:10:09.390
we have one more assumption
we show you later.

00:10:09.391 --> 00:10:13.653
And the CloudBerry mediaware
that's a lot of techniques

00:10:13.654 --> 00:10:16.583
optimization is to
deliver that kind of

00:10:16.584 --> 00:10:20.090
view experiences which
I'll explain later.

00:10:20.091 --> 00:10:21.630
&gt;&gt; You allow joint queries.

00:10:21.631 --> 00:10:24.811
&gt;&gt; We allow joint queries,
we support all kinds of queries.

00:10:24.812 --> 00:10:26.612
Some of [INAUDIBLE]
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE]

00:10:26.613 --> 00:10:28.084
&gt;&gt; Okay, I'll talk about the

00:10:28.085 --> 00:10:32.120
front end library, the front-end
API here, we use a RESTful API.

00:10:32.121 --> 00:10:36.130
But the RESTful API is kind of
a SQL, using a JSON format.

00:10:37.300 --> 00:10:38.760
Okay, I use this one here.

00:10:40.240 --> 00:10:44.040
So for Asterix DB, I think
I'll just talk about briefly,

00:10:44.041 --> 00:10:45.280
Mike and
I have been building, and

00:10:45.281 --> 00:10:49.043
some other colleagues from
Riverside, other Couchbase.

00:10:49.044 --> 00:10:53.399
We've been building this one for
last seven years.

00:10:53.400 --> 00:10:57.513
At the high level,
it combines the techniques from

00:10:57.514 --> 00:11:02.681
semistructure data management,
parallel database systems,

00:11:02.682 --> 00:11:07.847
and Hadoop and its friends into
one system, it's open source in

00:11:07.848 --> 00:11:13.650
Java, essentially it's a power
databases with all the support.

00:11:13.651 --> 00:11:17.780
Storage, indexing, clear
processing, clear optimization,

00:11:17.781 --> 00:11:19.842
clear language,
the whole stack, okay?

00:11:19.843 --> 00:11:22.396
There's a shared
[INAUDIBLE] architecture,

00:11:22.397 --> 00:11:25.822
we dont take the other approach
it's like an Oracle is taking

00:11:25.823 --> 00:11:29.383
some of their products, we just
assume people kept throwing in

00:11:29.384 --> 00:11:33.230
some commodities hardware
easily to make it scalable.

00:11:33.231 --> 00:11:36.245
These are some of
features AsterixDB has.

00:11:36.246 --> 00:11:39.861
Semi-structured data models,
will support ptree,

00:11:39.862 --> 00:11:45.920
rtree invert index all of them,
that covers a lot of data types.

00:11:45.921 --> 00:11:48.700
We use RCM For dynamic data.

00:11:48.701 --> 00:11:51.830
Similar to other
systems like Mongo and.

00:11:51.831 --> 00:11:56.200
And, we have our own runtime
engine called Hyrex.

00:11:56.201 --> 00:11:58.010
We have our own query language.

00:11:58.011 --> 00:12:00.340
Now, previously it's called AQL.

00:12:00.341 --> 00:12:02.300
Coming from a x query.

00:12:02.301 --> 00:12:05.770
Now, people that don't like
x query People like SQL, so

00:12:05.771 --> 00:12:07.800
we change it to SQL Plus Plus.

00:12:07.801 --> 00:12:11.770
The plus plus part supports
same structured part, okay?

00:12:13.030 --> 00:12:16.830
And we are being working with
Couchbase very closely and

00:12:16.831 --> 00:12:22.220
the recent announcement from
them says, Couchbase decided to

00:12:22.221 --> 00:12:27.455
use the backend AsterixDB
to support their analytics.

00:12:27.456 --> 00:12:32.830
That's a one-minute
overview of AsterixDB.

